A||||3||rodmcneill||Buying Canadian Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||12-07-2001||12:24 AM||||I live in Indiana, what is involved in buying a Canadian Fiero? Any weird paperwork-time involved? Is it easier to buy it on this side of the border?||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||12-07-2001||07:36 AM||||If memeory servs me correct, buying a Canadian car is just like buying a Florida car.  Buy it, get a good bill of sale, title signed, whatever and drive to the border.  Show the paperwork and your I.D.  Drive thru.  <P>Go to your local registration office and do whatever you would have to do with that Florida car.<P>You're done!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou||12-07-2001||08:09 AM||||Here in Florida a Title and notarized bill of sale is required and if the car is from out of the USA it has to have a manufacturers statement of emissions compliance , be inspected for the original emissions equipment to be in place and be able to pass emissions as well. As a matter of fact I believe that Alabama is the only state to issue a title on a vehicle with just a bill of sale and even then it has to be older than 1976.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jay||12-07-2001||06:21 PM||||Yes what Lou said is correct.  A compliance letter from GM Canada stating the car meets EPA and DOT saftey standards.  The owner calls GM and gives them the VIN # and they ship it out.  It costs around $70Canadain for the letter.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig2_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
